$AB \parallel CD$ means line $AB$ is **parallel** to line $CD$, staying the same distance away.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw (-2.4,1.1) -- (2.4,1.1);
\node[left] at (-2.4,1.1) {$A$};
\node[right] at (2.4,1.1) {$B$};
\draw (-2.4,-1.1) -- (2.4,-1.1);
\node[left] at (-2.4,-1.1) {$C$};
\node[right] at (2.4,-1.1) {$D$};
\draw[->] (-0.35,1.1) -- (0.35,1.1);
\draw[->] (-0.35,-1.1) -- (0.35,-1.1);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

$EF \perp GH$ means line $EF$ is **perpendicular** to line $GH$, meeting it at $90^{\circ}$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{calc}
\coordinate (X) at (0,0);
\coordinate (E) at (-2.2,0);
\coordinate (F) at (2.2,0);
\coordinate (G) at (0,-1.9);
\coordinate (H) at (0,1.9);
\draw (E) -- (F);
\draw (G) -- (H);
\coordinate (p) at ($(X)!0.32cm!(F)$);
\coordinate (q) at ($(X)!0.32cm!(H)$);
\draw (p) -- ($(p)+(q)-(X)$) -- (q);
\node[left] at (E) {$E$};
\node[right] at (F) {$F$};
\node[below] at (G) {$G$};
\node[above] at (H) {$H$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Matching arrowhead marks identify parallel lines, and a different number of arrowheads identifies a different parallel set.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
